<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the generated manifest by the Android Gradle Plugin, which reads
  build-type source sets (src/release, src/debug) at a higher priority than
  src/main. scripts/android-overrides.mjs drops it into both, so the generated
  manifest is never edited — the file Capacitor rewrites stays the file
  Capacitor owns.

  Almost nothing needs to be here. @capacitor/local-notifications ships its own
  manifest declaring POST_NOTIFICATIONS, SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM, WAKE_LOCK,
  RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and the boot receiver that re-arms alarms after a
  restart; all of it arrives through the same merge. INTERNET comes from
  Capacitor's own template.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The one addition, and it earns its place.

      From Android 14, SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is denied by default to a newly
      installed app. The plugin's response is to fling the user out of the
      WebView into the system "Alarms & reminders" screen on the very first
      schedule() call — mid-tap, unannounced. USE_EXACT_ALARM is granted at
      install and cannot be revoked, so the reminder is simply exact and the
      ambush never happens.

      Google Play restricts this permission to alarm and calendar apps, which
      would rule it out — but that is a store review rule, enforced at
      submission, and this APK is sideloaded from a GitHub release. If PIT
      ever goes to Play, this line comes out and the settings-redirect flow
      comes back.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM" />

    <!--
      The other one, and it is easy to miss because nothing asks for it.

      Android's WebView only starts Chromium's network-change notifier when
      the host app holds this permission. Without it `navigator.onLine` is
      pinned to `true` forever and the `online` / `offline` events never
      fire — which would not be a cosmetic loss here. PIT's whole offline
      layer is built on those: `lib/sync.ts` drains its queue of unsent days
      on `online`, `lib/useCached.ts` revalidates on reconnect, and
      `SyncStatus` would sit there claiming a connection the phone does not
      have. The app would look like it had saved a day it was still holding.

      A normal, install-time permission: nothing is prompted, and it grants
      no ability to read the network, only to be told when it changes.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

</manifest>
